{"kind":"request","text":"Make the browser's work counts describe a population a reader can check, because today they describe two at once and the total corresponds to nothing.\n\nHugh reported this as \"the browser UI shows the wrong numbers\" and asked for it to be found and fixed. It replaces 08c5f7d4, which is stale: that request named the same disagreement, left the resolution open as a product judgement, and has since lost its basis. This one carries the decision, because Hugh's instruction settles the open question — the numbers should be right, not merely labelled.\n\nMeasured against the live workroom. The header chip in TopBar renders `+{summary.stale}` with the tooltip \"N need attention\", and that N is 413. Nothing in the system is 413. It is 223 commitments carrying a stale or disputed qualifier, plus 190 retired-or-stale artifacts, added together. `gs status` never adds those two: it reports commitments by status on one line and artifacts on another.\n\nTwo separate faults produce it. The first is the mixing: `workAttentionCount` sums commitments and artifacts, and the Work drawer's Attention filter repeats the sum inline. The second is subtler and predates it — Attention is not a third slice beside Active and Closed. Staleness and dispute are qualifiers sitting on top of a lifecycle status, so 188 of those 223 commitments are already counted as active or closed. The three figures therefore exceed the number of commitments, which is what makes a reader conclude one of the two surfaces must be miscounting. Neither is.\n\nThere is a third, smaller inconsistency in the same place: Active is counted from the projection while Attention and Closed are summed over topics, so a commitment whose root is not a topic is inside one number and outside the others.\n\nStatus does partition cleanly, and that is what makes the fix available rather than a compromise: 75 active, 411 closed and 35 lifecycle-stale sum to exactly the 521 commitments in the log.","body":{"conditions":"The browser's work counts come from one source and one rule, and describe one population: active, closed and lifecycle-stale partition commitments by status and sum to the commitment total, verifiable against `gs status`; attention is reported as the qualifier it is rather than as a third slice, so it may overlap the others and is labelled to say so; artifacts needing attention are reported beside the commitment counts rather than added to them, keeping the signal the drawer exists to surface while matching how the CLI separates the two; the header chip's tooltip states both figures rather than one mixed number; a test pins that the partition sums to the total and that attention never exceeds the population it qualifies; any existing test that encoded the mixed count is changed deliberately with the reason recorded rather than adjusted until it passes; `npx tsc --noEmit` and the UI tests pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}